const faqs = [
  { q: 'What are the two pillars of the nominal sentence?', a: 'The mubtadaʾ (the subject of predication) and the khabar (the predicate). Both are marfūʿ — in the nominative. In al-ʿilmu nūrun ("knowledge is light"), al-ʿilmu is mubtadaʾ marfūʿ and nūrun is khabar marfūʿ. Neither is governed by a verb, because there is no verb in the sentence at all.' },
  { q: 'Why is there no word for "is" in an Arabic nominal sentence?', a: 'Arabic does not need a copula in the present affirmative. The relationship between mubtadaʾ and khabar is expressed by juxtaposition plus the shared nominative case, not by a linking verb. English must supply "is" in translation, but nothing in the Arabic corresponds to it — which is why looking for a verb to explain the case ending will always fail here.' },
  { q: 'Can a nominal sentence begin with an indefinite noun?', a: 'The default is that the mubtadaʾ is definite, because a statement about something wholly unidentified conveys nothing. It may be indefinite when something makes it informative (a musawwigh): if it is described (rajulun karīmun zāranā), preceded by negation or a question (mā aḥadun ʿindanā), expresses a prayer (salāmun ʿalaykum), or the predicate is a fronted prepositional phrase (fī l-dāri rajulun).' },
  { q: 'What are the three kinds of khabar?', a: 'Singular/simple (al-ʿilmu nūrun), a full sentence — nominal or verbal — which must contain a rābiṭ (a link, usually a pronoun) referring back to the mubtadaʾ (al-ḥadīqatu azhāruhā jamīlatun), and a shibh jumla: a prepositional phrase or an adverbial (al-kitābu fawqa l-ṭāwilati).' },
  { q: 'What happens when kāna or inna enters the sentence?', a: 'They are nawāsikh — "abrogators" that cancel the default marfūʿ/marfūʿ pattern. Kāna and its sisters keep the first pillar marfūʿ (now called ism kāna) and put the predicate in the accusative (khabar kāna manṣūb). Inna and its sisters do the opposite: the first pillar becomes manṣūb (ism inna) and the predicate stays marfūʿ.' },
  { q: 'Is al-ṭālibu kataba a nominal or a verbal sentence?', a: 'Nominal, in the Baṣran analysis this site follows. The classification depends on what the sentence opens with, not on whether a verb appears in it. Because the noun comes first it is a mubtadaʾ, and the verbal sentence that follows occupies the position of khabar (fī maḥall rafʿ khabar).' },
];
