  title="The Nominal Sentence (al-jumla al-ismiyya) in Arabic Grammar · Irab App"
  description="A full lesson on al-jumla al-ismiyya: its two pillars mubtadaʾ and khabar, why both are marfūʿ without any verb, the three kinds of khabar, fronting the predicate, indefinite subjects, and the abrogators — with complete classical iʿrāb and Quranic evidence."
>
  <ArticleSchema slot="head" lang="en" kind="lesson" headline="The Nominal Sentence (al-jumla al-ismiyya)" description="A full lesson on al-jumla al-ismiyya: its two pillars mubtadaʾ and khabar, why both are marfūʿ without any verb, the three kinds of khabar, fronting the predicate, indefinite subjects, and the abrogators — with complete classical iʿrāb and Quranic evidence." />
  <Nav lang="en" />

  <ArticleHero
    lang="en"
    title="The Nominal Sentence"
    summary="Arabic builds statements in two ways, and everything in naḥw follows from which one you are looking at. The nominal sentence — al-jumla al-ismiyya — opens with a noun and rests on two pillars, the mubtadaʾ and the khabar, both in the nominative. This lesson explains what puts them there when there is no verb in sight, the three shapes the predicate can take, when the order reverses, and what happens when an abrogator enters. Every rule is followed by a complete iʿrāb in the wording the classical books use."
  />
  <Byline lang="en" kind="lesson" />

  <ArticleSection
    lang="en"
    id="terms"
    title="The terms, before anything else"
    lead="Half the difficulty English speakers have with Arabic grammar comes from English labels that almost fit. Fix the vocabulary first and the rules become straightforward."
  >
    <ul>
      <li><strong>الجملة الاسمية</strong> — <em>al-jumla al-ismiyya</em>, "the nominal sentence": a sentence whose opening element is a noun.</li>
      <li><strong>المبتدأ</strong> — <em>al-mubtadaʾ</em>, literally "that which is begun with". Usually glossed <em>subject</em>. <strong>Handle with care:</strong> English also calls the doer of a verb the "subject", but Arabic gives that a different name entirely (<em>fāʿil</em>) in a different sentence type. A mubtadaʾ is not a fāʿil, and the two are never interchangeable.</li>
      <li><strong>الخبر</strong> — <em>al-khabar</em>, "the report, the information". Glossed <em>predicate</em>. It is what you are saying <em>about</em> the mubtadaʾ.</li>
      <li><strong>مرفوع</strong> — <em>marfūʿ</em>, "raised": the nominative state. Its default marker is <em>ḍamma</em> (‑u).</li>
      <li><strong>عامل</strong> — <em>ʿāmil</em>, "governor, operator": whatever causes a word to take the case it takes. Governance is the engine of the whole system, and section three is about the one that operates here.</li>
      <li><strong>رابط</strong> — <em>rābiṭ</em>, "link": the element that ties a sentence-predicate back to its subject.</li>
      <li><strong>نواسخ</strong> — <em>nawāsikh</em>, "abrogators": words that enter the nominal sentence and overturn its default case pattern.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Throughout these lessons Arabic terms are given in script, then transliterated, then glossed. The transliteration is academic — macrons mark long vowels, <em>ʿ</em> is ʿayn and <em>ʾ</em> is hamza — so that every form can be read back into Arabic without ambiguity.</p>
  </ArticleSection>

  <ArticleSection
    lang="en"
    id="definition"
    title="What makes a sentence nominal"
    lead="A sentence is nominal when the first of its original elements is a noun. That is the entire test — and it is a test about form, not about meaning."
  >
    <p>The grammarians classify by the <em>ṣadr</em>, the opening, of the sentence. <span class="ar" lang="ar" dir="rtl">مُحَمَّدٌ مُسَافِرٌ</span> — <em>Muḥammadun musāfirun</em>, "Muhammad is travelling" — is nominal because it opens with a noun.</p>
    <p>The case that surprises English speakers is <span class="ar" lang="ar" dir="rtl">الطَّالِبُ كَتَبَ الدَّرْسَ</span> — <em>al-ṭālibu kataba l-darsa</em>, "the student wrote the lesson". There is plainly a verb in it, yet the sentence is <strong>nominal</strong>, because a noun came first. The noun is therefore a mubtadaʾ, and the verbal sentence following it occupies the slot of the khabar. Compare <span class="ar" lang="ar" dir="rtl">كَتَبَ الطَّالِبُ الدَّرْسَ</span> — <em>kataba l-ṭālibu l-darsa</em> — which is <a href="/en/duroos/al-jumla-al-filiyya">verbal</a>, opens with the verb, and in which <em>al-ṭālibu</em> is a fāʿil rather than a mubtadaʾ. Same words, same meaning in translation, two different analyses.</p>
    <p>This classification is Baṣran, and it is the one the mainstream tradition and this site follow. The Kūfans analysed fronted-noun sentences differently in places; where such disagreements affect the iʿrāb you will meet them in the relevant lesson.</p>
    <p>Semantically the tradition observes that the nominal sentence tends to express <em>thubūt</em> — stability, a settled state — while the verbal sentence expresses <em>tajaddud</em>, renewal and occurrence. This is a tendency worth knowing, not a rule to parse by. Parse by the opening element.</p>
  </ArticleSection>

  <ArticleSection
    lang="en"
    id="the-two-pillars"
    title="The two pillars, and what raises them"
    lead="Both mubtadaʾ and khabar are marfūʿ. The interesting question — the one most textbooks skip — is what puts them in the nominative when the sentence contains no verb to govern anything."
  >
    <p>Take <span class="ar" lang="ar" dir="rtl">الْعِلْمُ نُورٌ</span> — <em>al-ʿilmu nūrun</em>, "knowledge is light".</p>
    <p><strong>The iʿrāb, in the classical wording:</strong> <span class="ar" lang="ar" dir="rtl">العلمُ مبتدأ مرفوع وعلامة رفعه الضمة الظاهرة على آخره، نورٌ خبر مرفوع وعلامة رفعه الضمة الظاهرة على آخره.</span></p>
    <p><strong>Decoded:</strong> <em>al-ʿilmu</em> is mubtadaʾ, marfūʿ, and the marker of its nominative is the visible ḍamma on its final letter; <em>nūrun</em> is khabar, marfūʿ, and the marker of its nominative is the visible ḍamma on its final letter. Notice the shape of the statement: what the word <em>is</em>, what case it is <em>in</em>, and <em>which marker</em> shows that case, plus why that marker and not another. Every iʿrāb line you will ever read has these parts.</p>
    <p>Now the governance question. In a verbal sentence the verb is the ʿāmil — it raises its fāʿil. Here there is no verb. The grammarians' answer is that the mubtadaʾ is raised by <em>al-ibtidāʾ</em> itself: by the bare fact of being the element the sentence is built on. This is a <em>ʿāmil maʿnawī</em>, an abstract governor rather than a word you can point to. The khabar in turn is raised by the mubtadaʾ. So the chain runs: initiality raises the subject, and the subject raises the predicate.</p>
    <p>This matters practically. Students who have learned "the verb causes the endings" go looking for a verb in <em>al-ʿilmu nūrun</em>, fail to find one, and guess. There is nothing to find: the governor here is a relationship, not a word.</p>
    <p>A second example, with a sound masculine plural: <span class="ar" lang="ar" dir="rtl">الْمُؤْمِنُونَ صَادِقُونَ</span> — <em>al-muʾminūna ṣādiqūna</em>, "the believers are truthful". <strong>Iʿrāb:</strong> <span class="ar" lang="ar" dir="rtl">المؤمنون مبتدأ مرفوع وعلامة رفعه الواو لأنه جمع مذكر سالم، صادقون خبر مرفوع وعلامة رفعه الواو لأنه جمع مذكر سالم.</span> Both are still marfūʿ, but the marker is <em>wāw</em> rather than ḍamma, because a sound masculine plural takes letters as case markers instead of vowels — see <a href="/en/duroos/alamat-al-irab">the markers of iʿrāb</a>. The case never changed; only its outward sign did.</p>
  </ArticleSection>

  <ArticleSection
    lang="en"
    id="english-contrast"
    title="What English can and cannot lend you"
    lead="English has almost no case system left, but the fragment it retains is exactly the right analogy — and there is one habit it will actively mislead you into."
  >
    <p>English marks case on pronouns only: <em>he</em> versus <em>him</em>, <em>who</em> versus <em>whom</em>. Nobody thinks "him" means something different from "he"; it is the same word wearing a different grammatical role. Arabic simply does openly, on nearly every noun, what English does on a handful of pronouns. When you read <em>al-ʿilmu</em> and later <em>al-ʿilma</em>, that final vowel is doing the work <em>he/him</em> does.</p>
    <p>The habit to unlearn is supplying "is". "Knowledge <strong>is</strong> light" needs a verb in English; the Arabic has none, and no word in it corresponds to "is". If you look for the copula you will invent a governor that is not there. The equational relationship is carried by the two nouns standing together, both in the nominative.</p>
    <p>Learners who know Latin, German, Greek or Russian have a real advantage here and should lean on it: marfūʿ ≈ nominative, manṣūb ≈ accusative, majrūr ≈ genitive. The correspondence is close enough to be useful and loose enough to be dangerous, so treat it as a bridge to intuition rather than a definition.</p>
  </ArticleSection>

  <ArticleCta lang="en">
    <span slot="title">Parse a sentence of your own.</span>
    Paste any Arabic nominal sentence into the analyzer and watch this lesson applied word by word: the mubtadaʾ and its marker, the kind of khabar, and the link if the predicate is a sentence.
  </ArticleCta>

  <ArticleSection
    lang="en"
    id="types-of-khabar"
    title="The three shapes of the khabar"
    lead="The predicate can be a single word, a whole sentence, or a prepositional or adverbial phrase. Recognising which one you are looking at is most of the work of parsing a nominal sentence."
  >
    <p><strong>1. Mufrad (single).</strong> <span class="ar" lang="ar" dir="rtl">الصِّدْقُ فَضِيلَةٌ</span> — <em>al-ṣidqu faḍīlatun</em>, "truthfulness is a virtue". <strong>Iʿrāb:</strong> <span class="ar" lang="ar" dir="rtl">الصدقُ مبتدأ مرفوع وعلامة رفعه الضمة الظاهرة، فضيلةٌ خبر مرفوع وعلامة رفعه الضمة الظاهرة.</span> Note that <em>mufrad</em> here is a technical term meaning "not a sentence and not a phrase" — a dual or a plural still counts as mufrad in this sense.</p>
    <p><strong>2. Jumla (a sentence).</strong> <span class="ar" lang="ar" dir="rtl">الْحَدِيقَةُ أَزْهَارُهَا جَمِيلَةٌ</span> — <em>al-ḥadīqatu azhāruhā jamīlatun</em>, "the garden, its flowers are beautiful". <strong>Iʿrāb:</strong> <span class="ar" lang="ar" dir="rtl">الحديقةُ مبتدأ أول مرفوع، أزهارُ مبتدأ ثانٍ مرفوع وهو مضاف، والهاء ضمير متصل مبني في محل جر بالإضافة، جميلةٌ خبر المبتدأ الثاني مرفوع، والجملة الاسمية من المبتدأ الثاني وخبره في محل رفع خبر المبتدأ الأول، والرابط الضمير.</span></p>
    <p><strong>Decoded:</strong> a complete nominal sentence (<em>azhāruhā jamīlatun</em>) is itself serving as the predicate of <em>al-ḥadīqatu</em>. The phrase <em>fī maḥall rafʿ</em> — "in the position of nominative" — is how the tradition assigns a case to something that cannot physically carry a vowel: a whole sentence has no final letter to mark, but it occupies a slot that is nominative. And the sentence-predicate must contain a <strong>rābiṭ</strong> tying it to the subject; here it is the pronoun <em>‑hā</em> in <em>azhāruhā</em>, "its flowers". Without that link the two halves would be unrelated statements sitting side by side.</p>
    <p><strong>3. Shibh jumla (quasi-sentence).</strong> <span class="ar" lang="ar" dir="rtl">الْكِتَابُ فَوْقَ الطَّاوِلَةِ</span> — <em>al-kitābu fawqa l-ṭāwilati</em>, "the book is on the table". <strong>Iʿrāb:</strong> <span class="ar" lang="ar" dir="rtl">الكتابُ مبتدأ مرفوع وعلامة رفعه الضمة الظاهرة، فوقَ ظرف مكان منصوب وعلامة نصبه الفتحة الظاهرة وهو مضاف، الطاولةِ مضاف إليه مجرور وعلامة جره الكسرة الظاهرة، وشبه الجملة متعلق بمحذوف خبر.</span></p>
    <p><strong>Decoded, and this is the subtle one:</strong> the tradition does not say the phrase <em>is</em> the predicate. It says the phrase is <em>mutaʿalliq bi-maḥdhūf khabar</em> — "attached to an omitted predicate". The reasoning: a predicate must be a noun or a sentence, and a prepositional phrase is neither, so an implied predicate such as <em>kāʾinun</em> ("being, located") is posited and the phrase attaches to it. English translations quietly collapse this, but the omitted element is what the analysis actually rests on, and it will reappear in many later lessons.</p>
  </ArticleSection>

  <ArticleSection
    lang="en"
    id="word-order"
    title="Reversing the order, and starting with an indefinite"
    lead="The default is mubtadaʾ then khabar. The order may reverse — sometimes optionally, sometimes obligatorily — and one of those obligatory cases is also what licenses an indefinite subject."
  >
    <p>Fronting the khabar is <strong>obligatory</strong> in three well-known situations:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>The khabar is a shibh jumla and the mubtadaʾ is indefinite: <span class="ar" lang="ar" dir="rtl">فِي الدَّارِ رَجُلٌ</span> — <em>fī l-dāri rajulun</em>, "in the house is a man".</li>
      <li>The khabar has inherent right to initial position, such as an interrogative: <span class="ar" lang="ar" dir="rtl">أَيْنَ الْكِتَابُ؟</span> — <em>ayna l-kitābu</em>, "where is the book?"</li>
      <li>The mubtadaʾ carries a pronoun referring to something inside the khabar: <span class="ar" lang="ar" dir="rtl">فِي الدَّارِ صَاحِبُهَا</span> — <em>fī l-dāri ṣāḥibuhā</em>, "in the house is its owner". Leaving the order unreversed would make the pronoun refer forward to something not yet mentioned.</li>
    </ul>
    <p><strong>Iʿrāb of the first:</strong> <span class="ar" lang="ar" dir="rtl">في حرف جر مبني على السكون لا محل له من الإعراب، الدارِ اسم مجرور بفي وعلامة جره الكسرة الظاهرة، والجار والمجرور متعلقان بمحذوف خبر مقدم، رجلٌ مبتدأ مؤخر مرفوع وعلامة رفعه الضمة الظاهرة.</span> — <em>fī</em> is a preposition, indeclinable, with no position in iʿrāb; <em>al-dāri</em> is genitive by it; the prepositional phrase attaches to an omitted <strong>fronted</strong> predicate; and <em>rajulun</em> is the <strong>postponed</strong> subject, nominative.</p>
    <p>Which brings us to indefinite subjects. The default is a definite mubtadaʾ, for a plain reason: a statement about something entirely unidentified conveys nothing — "a man is tall" tells a listener nothing at all. An indefinite is permitted when something makes it informative, and the tradition catalogues these licensors (<em>musawwighāt</em>):</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>It is described:</strong> <span class="ar" lang="ar" dir="rtl">رَجُلٌ كَرِيمٌ زَارَنَا</span> — <em>rajulun karīmun zāranā</em>, "a generous man visited us".</li>
      <li><strong>It follows negation or a question:</strong> <span class="ar" lang="ar" dir="rtl">مَا أَحَدٌ عِنْدَنَا</span> — <em>mā aḥadun ʿindanā</em>, "no one is with us".</li>
      <li><strong>It expresses a prayer:</strong> <span class="ar" lang="ar" dir="rtl">سَلَامٌ عَلَيْكُمْ</span> — <em>salāmun ʿalaykum</em>, "peace be upon you".</li>
      <li><strong>The predicate is fronted:</strong> the <em>fī l-dāri rajulun</em> case above.</li>
    </ul>
  </ArticleSection>

  <ArticleSection
    lang="en"
    id="nawasikh"
    title="The abrogators: when the pattern is overturned"
    lead="Certain verbs and particles enter the nominal sentence and cancel its default marfūʿ/marfūʿ pattern. They are called nawāsikh — abrogators — and the two families pull in opposite directions."
  >
    <p><strong>Kāna and its sisters</strong> leave the first pillar nominative and put the predicate in the accusative. <span class="ar" lang="ar" dir="rtl">كَانَ الْجَوُّ صَحْوًا</span> — <em>kāna l-jawwu ṣaḥwan</em>, "the weather was clear". <strong>Iʿrāb:</strong> <span class="ar" lang="ar" dir="rtl">كان فعل ماضٍ ناقص مبني على الفتح، الجوُّ اسم كان مرفوع وعلامة رفعه الضمة الظاهرة، صحوًا خبر كان منصوب وعلامة نصبه الفتحة الظاهرة.</span> Note the renaming: the pillars are no longer "mubtadaʾ and khabar" but <em>ism kāna</em> and <em>khabar kāna</em>. Kāna is called <em>nāqiṣ</em>, "incomplete", because unlike an ordinary verb it does not describe an event — it contributes tense and requires a predicate to complete it.</p>
    <p><strong>Inna and its sisters</strong> do the reverse: accusative on the first pillar, nominative on the predicate. <span class="ar" lang="ar" dir="rtl">إِنَّ اللهَ غَفُورٌ رَحِيمٌ</span> — <em>inna Llāha ghafūrun raḥīmun</em>, "indeed Allah is Forgiving, Merciful". <strong>Iʿrāb:</strong> <span class="ar" lang="ar" dir="rtl">إنّ حرف توكيد ونصب مبني على الفتح لا محل له من الإعراب، اللهَ لفظ الجلالة اسم إنّ منصوب وعلامة نصبه الفتحة الظاهرة، غفورٌ خبر إنّ مرفوع وعلامة رفعه الضمة الظاهرة، رحيمٌ خبر ثانٍ مرفوع.</span></p>
    <p>The word <em>nāsikh</em> is worth pausing on: its root means to cancel or supersede. The grammarians are saying the sentence still <em>is</em> a nominal sentence with two pillars — the abrogator has overturned the case ruling on them, not rebuilt the sentence. Each family has its own conditions and meanings, treated in <a href="/en/duroos/kana-wa-akhawatuha">kāna and its sisters</a> and <a href="/en/duroos/inna-wa-akhawatuha">inna and its sisters</a>.</p>
  </ArticleSection>

  <ArticleSection
    lang="en"
    id="common-mistakes"
    title="Where English speakers go wrong"
    lead="Four errors account for most mistakes on this chapter, and three of them come specifically from carrying English assumptions across."
  >
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Putting the khabar in the accusative.</strong> Writing <em>al-ʿilmu nūran</em> by analogy with an object. The predicate is nominative always, unless an abrogator has changed it. There is no verb here to take an object.</li>
      <li><strong>Hunting for the missing "is".</strong> Because English needs a copula, learners look for a governor that does not exist and then cannot explain the endings. The governor is <em>al-ibtidāʾ</em>, an abstract one.</li>
      <li><strong>Forgetting the rābiṭ.</strong> When the predicate is a sentence it must contain a pronoun — visible or implied — pointing back at the subject. Without it you have two unrelated statements.</li>
      <li><strong>Confusing a postponed mubtadaʾ with a fāʿil.</strong> In <span class="ar" lang="ar" dir="rtl">فِي الصَّفِّ طَالِبٌ</span> — <em>fī l-ṣaffi ṭālibun</em>, "in the class is a student" — there is no verb in the sentence at all, so <em>ṭālibun</em> cannot possibly be a fāʿil. It is a postponed mubtadaʾ. A fāʿil requires a verb; no verb, no fāʿil, ever.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The cure for all four is the same: parse real sentences, out loud, in the full classical wording, until the shape of the iʿrāb line is automatic. Read the <a href="/en/irab">iʿrāb reference</a> for that wording, then take sentences from anything you are reading and parse them before checking yourself against the analyzer.</p>
  </ArticleSection>

  <ArticleSection
    lang="en"
    id="sources"
    title="Sources"
    lead="This lesson follows the mainstream Baṣran analysis as presented in the standard teaching texts. Where they differ from one another, the difference is noted in the lesson itself."
